"""Original thyroid chapter: nodules and contemporary risk-adapted oncology."""
from create_thyrotoxicosis import rows,q,NI,HT,PG,TH,MC,AM,GO
from qbank_authoring import ref,finalize
NO=ref('ETA Clinical Practice Guideline: Thyroid Nodule Management, 2023','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10448590/')
CA=ref('ATA Differentiated Thyroid Cancer Guideline, 2025','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13090833/')
ME=ref('ATA Medullary Thyroid Carcinoma Guideline, 2015','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4490627/')
q('A thyroid nodule is detected in an adult. Which initial combination is central to assessment?', 'Serum TSH and risk-stratifying neck ultrasound',[
 ('Serum thyroglobulin alone as a cancer screening test','It is not a sufficiently specific nodule cancer test.'),('Immediate total thyroidectomy for every nodule','Most nodules are benign and need risk-based assessment.'),('A normal TSH alone excludes thyroid malignancy','Function and malignancy risk are different questions.')],
 'Evaluate function and structure separately', '**TSH helps identify a possible autonomous nodule.**',['Ultrasound evaluates morphology, size and lymph nodes.','Clinical history includes radiation, family syndromes and compressive symptoms.'], '**Use a validated system to decide FNA and follow-up.**',['Do not biopsy every small low-risk nodule.','Suspicious nodes or invasion change the pathway.'],[['TSH','Function'],['Ultrasound','Structural risk'],['FNA decision','Risk pattern plus size/context']],[NO])
q('A nonpregnant adult has a thyroid nodule and suppressed TSH. Which additional test often precedes an FNA decision?', 'Thyroid scintigraphy to assess whether the nodule is hyperfunctioning',[
 ('Serum thyroglobulin as a definitive malignancy test','It does not determine the functional status or reliably diagnose cancer.'),('FNA of every nodule without considering autonomy','Functional assessment can avoid unnecessary biopsy of an autonomous lesion.'),('No further assessment because a low TSH proves benign anatomy','The structural and functional context still matters.')],
 'A low TSH changes the diagnostic sequence', '**Scintigraphy identifies autonomous hormone production.**',['A hyperfunctioning nodule corresponding to the lesion usually does not require routine FNA.','Discordant suspicious findings still need appropriate review.'], '**Avoid radionuclide testing in pregnancy.**',['Evaluate the degree of thyrotoxicosis.','Use the full ultrasound and clinical picture.'],[['Low TSH','Consider scintigraphy'],['Corresponding hot nodule','Usually no routine FNA'],['Exceptions','Discordant or concerning context']],[NO],'clinical_scenario')
q('Which ultrasound pattern raises thyroid malignancy concern more than a simple spongiform pattern?', 'A solid markedly hypoechoic nodule with irregular margins and punctate echogenic foci',[
 ('A purely cystic lesion without suspicious components','This is generally low risk.'),('A typical spongiform nodule without other warning signs','This is usually low risk.'),('A normal-appearing lymph node with preserved hilum','This is not a suspicious nodule pattern.')],
 'Combine features rather than using size alone', '**Margins, echogenicity, shape and echogenic foci contribute to risk classification.**',['No single feature is perfectly diagnostic.','Suspicious lymph nodes can override a simple size-based pathway.'], '**Apply one validated ultrasound system consistently.**',['FNA size cutoffs differ between systems.','Avoid mixing a score from one system with a cutoff from another.'],[['Higher-risk pattern','Hypoechoic/irregular/punctate foci'],['Lower-risk pattern','Typical spongiform or simple cyst'],['Decision','Validated risk system']],[NO])
q('Cytology reports a follicular neoplasm. Why can FNA not usually distinguish follicular adenoma from carcinoma?', 'Capsular or vascular invasion requires histological assessment of the lesion architecture',[
 ('Follicular carcinoma never forms a discrete lesion','It can present as a nodule.'),('All follicular-pattern cytology is definitely malignant','Benign adenomas can have similar cellular features.'),('A normal TSH excludes follicular malignancy','Most nodules are not diagnosed by function alone.')],
 'Some malignant criteria are architectural rather than purely cytological', '**FNA samples cells but not the entire tumor-capsule interface.**',['Risk assessment and molecular testing can refine selected decisions.','The appropriate diagnostic surgery depends on the complete context.'], '**Explain uncertainty rather than labeling all indeterminate nodules cancer.**',['Use local test performance and availability when considering molecular assays.','Final histology can change the extent of subsequent treatment.'],[['FNA','Cell pattern'],['Carcinoma criterion','Capsular/vascular invasion'],['Assessment','Architecture and integrated risk']],[NO,CA])
q('A thyroid tumor has neuroendocrine morphology and elevated calcitonin. Which origin is most characteristic?', 'Parafollicular C cells',[
 ('Thyroid follicular cells producing thyroglobulin','That is the origin of differentiated follicular-cell cancers.'),('Parathyroid chief cells','These produce PTH and represent a different tumor group.'),('Pituitary thyrotrophs','These are not located within the thyroid tumor.')],
 'Medullary carcinoma differs biologically from differentiated thyroid cancer', '**Calcitonin and CEA are important markers in medullary disease.**',['Germline RET assessment can identify hereditary disease.','Radioiodine is not a standard effective treatment for nonfollicular C-cell cancer.'], '**Evaluate hereditary implications and associated tumors.**',['Family counseling can be crucial.','Use stage and molecular findings to guide specialist management.'],[['Origin','C cells'],['Markers','Calcitonin and CEA'],['Hereditary pathway','RET/MEN2 assessment']],[ME])
q('A patient with medullary thyroid carcinoma has a germline RET pathogenic variant. What must be addressed before thyroid surgery?', 'Screen for pheochromocytoma and treat an identified pheochromocytoma first with appropriate preparation',[
 ('Proceed directly because a thyroid lesion cannot coexist with adrenal disease','MEN2 can include pheochromocytoma.'),('Start a beta blocker alone for suspected pheochromocytoma','Unopposed alpha stimulation can be dangerous.'),('Radioiodine eliminates the need for MEN2 evaluation','Medullary disease is not managed that way.')],
 'A hereditary diagnosis changes perioperative sequencing', '**An unrecognized catecholamine tumor can cause a severe surgical crisis.**',['Biochemical screening uses plasma free or urinary fractionated metanephrines as appropriate.','Hyperparathyroidism assessment is relevant in MEN2A.'], '**Coordinate endocrine and surgical management.**',['Treat the adrenal risk before elective thyroid surgery when present.','Provide genetic counseling and family testing.'],[['RET-associated disease','Consider MEN2'],['Preoperative hazard','Pheochromocytoma'],['Sequence','Address catecholamine tumor first']],[ME],'clinical_scenario')
q('A 1.8-cm unilateral papillary thyroid cancer has no gross extrathyroidal extension, no metastases and no contralateral indication for removal. Which initial operation aligns with ATA 2025 guidance?', 'Thyroid lobectomy',[
 ('Mandatory total thyroidectomy plus radioiodine for every patient','The updated guideline supports a less extensive operation in this low-risk setting.'),('Routine radical neck dissection without nodal disease','This is not indicated by the described staging.'),('No discussion of surgical or surveillance options is ever needed','Management requires informed risk-adapted planning.')],
 'The extent of surgery should match disease risk', '**ATA 2025 recommends lobectomy for suitable cancers at most 2 cm without gross extension or metastases.**',['Bilateral disease or another contralateral indication can change the choice.','Higher-risk findings may later require completion surgery.'], '**Discuss benefits and trade-offs before treatment.**',['Lobectomy can reduce complication burden.','Follow-up and the possibility of additional treatment must be explained.'],[['Size','1.8 cm'],['Extent','Unilateral, no gross extension/metastasis'],['Recommended initial operation','Lobectomy in this context']],[CA],'clinical_scenario')
q('An 8-mm low-risk papillary thyroid cancer is intrathyroidal, remote from critical structures and has no nodal disease. A reliable patient prefers observation. Which option is reasonable?', 'Active surveillance in an experienced program after informed discussion',[
 ('Every subcentimeter papillary cancer requires immediate total thyroidectomy','Selected low-risk microcarcinomas can be observed.'),('Observation means no follow-up imaging or review','Active surveillance is structured monitoring.'),('Surveillance is equally suitable with invasion or unreliable follow-up','Those features can make it inappropriate.')],
 'Active surveillance is an intentional treatment strategy', '**Selection depends on tumor anatomy, risk and the ability to follow up.**',['Progression, nodal disease, concerning growth or preference can prompt surgery.','Anxiety and life circumstances affect suitability.'], '**Use explicit follow-up and intervention criteria.**',['Do not present surveillance as neglect.','Discuss surgery as a valid alternative with its own risks.'],[['Candidate','Selected low-risk microcarcinoma'],['Requirement','Reliable structured follow-up'],['Conversion','Progression or informed preference']],[CA],'clinical_scenario')
q('After complete surgery for a low-risk differentiated thyroid cancer, which statement about radioiodine is correct?', 'It is not automatically required for every low-risk patient',[
 ('Every thyroid cancer, including medullary cancer, benefits equally','Radioiodine biology and indication differ.'),('No high-risk differentiated cancer can ever benefit','Selected higher-risk disease can warrant treatment.'),('The decision depends only on the original nodule diameter','Histology, stage, residual disease and risk all matter.')],
 'Adjuvant therapy is risk adapted', '**Radioiodine selection considers recurrence risk and residual disease.**',['Low-risk patients can often avoid unnecessary exposure.','Pregnancy and breastfeeding create important contraindications.'], '**Integrate pathology and postoperative assessment.**',['Explain the intended purpose of any treatment.','Do not apply historical universal ablation to every modern low-risk case.'],[['Low risk','Often no routine RAI'],['Higher risk/residual disease','Individualized indication'],['Decision','Pathology and postoperative risk']],[CA])
q('A low-risk thyroid-cancer survivor has an excellent response but develops atrial fibrillation while TSH is markedly suppressed. What is the best principle?', 'Reassess the need for suppression and individualize the TSH goal to recurrence risk and treatment harm',[
 ('Maintain maximal suppression forever in every survivor','The 2025 guideline supports risk-adapted de-escalation.'),('TSH suppression has no cardiac or skeletal consequences','It can cause harm.'),('Stop all thyroid replacement abruptly','Replacement need and suppression intensity are separate decisions.')],
 'A cancer-related target is not necessarily lifelong', '**High-risk persistent disease may justify greater suppression than a low-risk excellent response.**',['Atrial fibrillation and bone loss affect the balance.','Follow-up response can change the initial risk assessment.'], '**Adjust dose under a clear surveillance plan.**',['Maintain appropriate replacement if the gland is absent.','Do not confuse a normal TSH target with stopping follow-up.'],[['Benefit','Depends on residual/recurrent risk'],['Harm','Cardiac and skeletal'],['Plan','Dynamic risk-based target']],[CA],'clinical_scenario')
q('A differentiated thyroid-cancer survivor has positive anti-thyroglobulin antibodies. How should a low measured thyroglobulin be interpreted?', 'With caution because antibodies can interfere; serial antibody trends and imaging may be needed',[
 ('It definitively proves absence of all disease','Antibody interference can make the result misleading.'),('The antibodies prove recurrent cancer in every case','Their presence alone is not a tissue diagnosis.'),('Use a different assay each visit without regard to comparability','Serial interpretation benefits from consistent methods.')],
 'A tumor marker is only useful when its analytical context is understood', '**TgAb can distort immunometric thyroglobulin measurements.**',['Use the same assay when following trends where possible.','Rising or falling antibodies require interpretation with the overall response.'], '**Combine biochemistry with structural surveillance.**',['The value of Tg also differs after total thyroidectomy versus lobectomy.','Do not investigate or reassure from one isolated confounded number.'],[['TgAb','Potential assay interference'],['Serial data','Prefer comparable methods'],['Assessment','Marker plus imaging/context']],[CA])
q('An older patient has a rapidly enlarging hard neck mass, dysphagia, hoarseness and airway symptoms. What is the management priority?', 'Urgent airway and tissue-diagnosis assessment with a multidisciplinary thyroid-cancer team',[
 ('Routine annual ultrasound follow-up only','Rapid progression and airway symptoms are red flags.'),('Assume a benign goiter because thyroid nodules are common','Aggressive malignancy or lymphoma requires urgent exclusion.'),('Radioiodine before tissue diagnosis for every rapidly growing mass','The tumor type determines treatment and many aggressive tumors are not iodine-responsive.')],
 'A threatening presentation overrides a routine nodule schedule', '**Anaplastic carcinoma, thyroid lymphoma and other invasive lesions are important considerations.**',['Tissue diagnosis and molecular profiling can rapidly influence options.','Airway planning must occur before deterioration.'], '**Coordinate surgery, oncology, pathology and airway expertise.**',['Biopsy method depends on the suspected lesion and safety.','Do not delay urgent assessment while repeating low-yield thyroid-function tests.'],[['Red flags','Rapid growth and compressive symptoms'],['Priority','Airway and diagnosis'],['Treatment','Histology- and stage-directed']],[NO,CA],'clinical_scenario')

